<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yaroslav the Wise</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 978–<a href="/years/1054">1054</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yaroslav the Wise was Grand Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> from 1019 to 1054, after a long war of succession among the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir the Great</a>. He defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> outside Kiev in 1036, built the Cathedral of Saint Sophia, founded the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslavl">Yaroslavl</a> and is credited with the first written law code of the Rus, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russkaya-pravda">Russkaya Pravda</a>. The marriages of his children linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus</a> to the royal houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and his division of the realm among his sons shaped its later principalities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise.jpg" alt="Yaroslav the Wise" width="320" height="279" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YaroslavWiseSeal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of Yaroslav's early years. He was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir the Great</a>, presumably by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rogneda-of-polotsk">Rogneda of Polotsk</a>, although his age as stated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a>, and as shown by the examination of his skeleton in the 1930s, would place him among Vladimir's youngest children. Some scholars have suggested that his mother was in fact Anna Porphyrogenita. In the Norse sagas he appears as Jarisleif the Lame, and his lameness was confirmed by the scientists who examined his remains.</p><p>Vladimir sent him to rule the lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a>, and in 1010 he was transferred to Novgorod. There he founded the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslavl">Yaroslavl</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>. In 1014 he refused to pay tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>. After Vladimir's death in July 1015 he fought a bloody war against his half-brother Sviatopolk I, during which his brothers Boris, Gleb and Svyatoslav were killed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> blames Sviatopolk.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Yaroslav defeated Sviatopolk in 1016, lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> to him and his Polish allies in 1018, and firmly established his rule there in 1019. In 1024 his brother Mstislav of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> defeated him, and the two divided the realm along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>. After Mstislav's death in 1036 Yaroslav ruled the whole of it. He relied on a Scandinavian alliance, married Ingegerd Olofsdotter, daughter of the Swedish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olof-skotkonung">Olof Skötkonung</a>, in 1019, and captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartu">Tartu</a> in 1030, renaming it Yuryev.</p><p>To defend against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> he built a line of forts, and after his decisive victory over them near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> in 1036 he sponsored the building of Saint Sophia Cathedral in 1037. In 1051 he had the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hilarion-of-kiev">Hilarion of Kiev</a> made metropolitan, challenging the practice of appointing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>. A Rus' fleet sent against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1043 defeated the Byzantine fleet but was nearly destroyed by a storm. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russkaya-pravda">Russkaya Pravda</a> was probably issued during his Novgorod years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Yaroslav's daughters married into foreign courts: Elisiv to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-hardrada">Harald Hardrada</a>, Anastasia to the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-i-of-hungary">Andrew I of Hungary</a>, and Anne to Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, who acted as regent for her son. Three of his sons, Iziaslav I, Sviatoslav II and Vsevolod I, reigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> in turn. He urged his sons to live in peace. His sarcophagus in Saint Sophia was opened in 1936 and again in 2009, when it was found to hold only a female skeleton, and his remains are regarded as lost.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yaroslav the Wise” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Towns grew rapidly: Kiev, Novgorod, Chernigov, Smolensk, Polotsk and Vladimir became centres of crafts and commerce, and Kiev under <b>Yaroslav the Wise</b> was one of the largest cities in Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>As the second city of Kievan Rus’, Novgorod was usually governed by a son of the grand prince of Kiev: Vladimir the Great and <b>Yaroslav the Wise</b> both ruled there before taking the throne in Kiev, and the stone Cathedral of Saint Sophia was built in 1045–1050.</blockquote><a href="/empire/novgorod-republic">Novgorod Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He gave the land its law: <b>Yaroslav the Wise</b> (r. 1019–1054) wrote the first part of the Russkaya Pravda, and his sons and his grandson Vladimir Monomakh added to it.</blockquote><a href="/societies/kievan-rus">Social Hierarchy of Kievan Rus'</a></li><li><blockquote>King Henry I of France, seeking a third wife further afield, married Anna of Kiev, a daughter of <b>Yaroslav the Wise</b> of Kievan Rus'.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-19">19 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1060 they displaced the Oghuz and Pechenegs north of the Black Sea, and in 1068 they defeated the sons of <b>Yaroslav the Wise</b> on the Alta river.</blockquote><a href="/state/cumania">Cumania</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the will of <b>Yaroslav the Wise</b> it passed in 1054 to his son Sviatoslav II, and the Lyubech conference of 1097 confirmed it to Sviatoslav's sons, founding the Olgovichi dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-chernigov">Principality of Chernigov</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Died in Vyshhorod, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.45,30.52&amp;year=1027" aria-label="Kyiv on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5247 411 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Yaroslav the Wise"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5697 681h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5697 681h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.45,30.52&amp;year=1027">What was here in 1027? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
